# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Blair Hamilton
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Background commands and `wait` — #2318, which was written down as the area
# that might not be askable at all.
#
# The doubt was right about the reason and wrong about the conclusion. The
# suite ships no expected output, so a case has to reproduce, and `repeats()`
# refuses one that does not — and most of job control is scheduling order,
# which is exactly what will not reproduce. Two background jobs racing to
# write, a `jobs` listing with its pids and its `Running`/`Done` states, and
# anything that depends on which of two processes finished first are all
# unaskable here and stay unaskable.
#
# What survives that cut is not nothing. A background job's *status*, once
# waited for, is a number the shell computed and not a race; `wait` with
# nothing to wait for is an answer; and `$!` can be used without being
# printed. That subset is deterministic in all five columns, and it is the
# part a script actually depends on — `cmd & … ; wait "$pid" || handle_it` is
# how a shell script parallelizes anything.
#
# Nothing here prints a pid or a job number. `$!` is used as an operand and
# as the left side of a comparison, never written out.
#
# Held out, and both are dialect answers:
#   `wait` on a pid already waited for — bash and dash remember the status
#     and give it again; zsh and ksh93 answer 127, having forgotten the child
#   `set -m` — bash and ksh93 take it silently, dash warns that there is no
#     tty and carries on, and zsh refuses and ends the script. Three answers
#     to one line, in a suite run with no terminal anywhere.
#
# Three that were held out because *we* were the odd one out are now in the
# file below, #2650, #2651 and #2657 having been fixed: `$!` is a number for a
# background job with no operating-system process, a job a `wait` has reported
# gives its number back so the next job takes it, and `fg` with a job in the
# table refuses instead of running it.
#
# What was added after that, and what it cost: the file now asks what may be
# *put* in the background — a pipeline, a group, a loop, the right side of an
# and-list, a redirected command, a bare builtin, a job started in a function
# and one started in a subshell — because each is a job with a status and none
# of them is a race once it has been waited for. It asks the operands `wait`
# takes, a job's descriptors, `jobs -p` and `jobs -l`, `nohup`, and the one
# thing about a pid that is common: a job the shell is still running answers
# `kill -0`, and stops once `wait` has reaped it.
#
# Held open rather than allowed to finish: four cases here ask about a job
# the shell has not finished with, and every one of them holds it open on a
# fifo nothing has written to yet rather than letting it exit and asking
# quickly. A job that has ended and not yet been reaped is a *window* and not
# a state — every reference reaps from its own SIGCHLD handler — so a case
# that asks about one is asking which side of a scheduling decision the run
# landed on. zsh answered three of these lines two ways, about one run in
# forty under load, which cost the whole suite its determinism (#2974). No
# `sleep` anywhere: a job that is alive because it has not got round to
# exiting yet is the same race with a longer fuse.
#
# Each gate is spelled the same way, and both halves of the spelling were
# paid for:
#
#   the job reads *a line*, with `head -n 1`, and the release *writes* one.
#     Releasing by closing the fifo and letting the reader see an end of file
#     hangs: caught once in forty full-file runs on this machine, with the
#     writer's open long returned, no writer left anywhere, and `cat` still
#     asleep in `read`. A line survives that — it is in the fifo's buffer
#     whatever happens to the end of file, and the reader stops on having it
#     rather than on the writer having gone.
#   the fifo is the reader's *operand* and not a redirection on the job.
#     `head -n 1 gate` is a job that is a process with a pid as soon as it
#     starts; `head -n 1 < gate` is a job whose redirection is opened first,
#     so `$!` named something that was not there to be signalled yet — which
#     is the pid the `kill -0` cases below need rather than the job. Every
#     reference forks first and opens in the child; this shell settles a pid
#     of zero at the blocking open so that `&` returns at all, and a job
#     standing there is now a target that is *there* like the reference's
#     fork is (#2994). The spelling is kept because it is the one the file
#     was measured on, and the redirection form is asked beside it rather
#     than instead of it.
#
# Still held out, and now with the file each went to: `kill` on a job
# specification, which dash refuses entirely (dash/jobs.tests); `disown`,
# which bash and zsh have and ksh93 and dash do not; `$!` before any
# background command, which is 0 in zsh and unset in the other three; and the
# status `jobs` gives a job specification against an empty table, which is
# 127 in zsh where bash and ksh93 say 1 and dash says 2 (both zsh/jobs.tests).
#
# And held out because no reference repeats it: `wait` on a job specification
# naming a job that has already ended. bash, ksh93 and dash keep a finished
# job in the table until it is waited for and answer with its status; zsh
# drops it once it has noticed and answers 127 with a diagnostic. That is a
# real zsh-only answer and it still cannot be measured, because zsh gives it
# only once it has got round to reaping — so the file asks the jobspec of
# `jobs` while the job is held open, and asks the status of the pid (#2974).
#
# Held out for the same reason, and it used to be filed as a dialect answer:
# `wait` with several operands. POSIX makes the status the last operand's and
# the whole panel gives that — *once the children are reaped*. Before they
# are, zsh reports the older job instead, whichever order the operands were
# written in, so `wait "$c" "$d"` and `wait "$d" "$c"` both answer with `$c`'s
# status. zsh/jobs.tests had that written down as "zsh reports the first
# operand", which it does not: it was the reap window, and holding the jobs
# open on fifos does not reach it either — 54 runs in 120 split under load
# (#2993). One operand is a fact and stays; several is not askable.

# Nothing to wait for is a success, not an error.
wait; printf 'wait with nothing -> %s\n' "$?"

# The status of a background command is the status it exited with, and it
# arrives through `wait` rather than through `$?` at the point it was started.
( exit 7 ) & wait $!; printf 'background status -> %s\n' "$?"
( exit 0 ) & wait $!; printf 'background zero -> %s\n' "$?"

# `wait` with no operand waits for all of them and is a success however they
# ended — the statuses are not folded into it.
true & wait; printf 'wait all -> %s\n' "$?"
( exit 9 ) & : ; wait; printf 'wait after another command -> %s\n' "$?"

# A background job writes to the same output as the shell, and waiting for it
# is what makes the order of that writing a fact rather than a race.
( printf 'from background\n' ) & wait

# Including through a substitution: the subshell that captures it waits for
# what it started before it yields its text.
p=$( { printf 'captured\n'; } & wait ); printf '[%s]\n' "$p"

# `$!` is the most recent background command, and it can be saved and waited
# for later. It is never printed: a pid does not reproduce, and a suite that
# ships no expected output cannot ask for one.
( exit 3 ) &
b=$!
wait "$b"; printf 'by pid -> %s\n' "$?"

# `$!` is a *number* whatever the job was made of, and a different one per
# job. Every column forks before a background body runs, so a subshell of
# builtins has a process there and reports its id; this shell has none and
# invented one rather than reporting the 0 that `kill` spends as the whole
# process group (#2650). Compared rather than printed, for the reason above.
( exit 5 ) & a=$!
( exit 6 ) & b=$!
printf 'background pid is a number -> %s\n' "$(( a > 0 && b > 0 ))"
printf 'two jobs, two pids -> %s\n' "$(( a != b ))"

# And the id belongs to *that* job: waiting for the older of the two reports
# what it exited with and not what the newer one did. Waited newest first, so
# a shell that answered both reads from one job would be caught by the second
# line rather than by neither.
wait "$b"; printf 'newer by pid -> %s\n' "$?"
wait "$a"; printf 'older by pid -> %s\n' "$?"

# `%1` is the first job the shell holds *now*, not the first it ever started.
# A job that has been waited for gives its number back, and the next job takes
# it — unanimous across the panel, and this shell went on answering with the
# first job's status under a number it should have let go of (#2651). The
# reaping is what makes the case discriminating: with no completed job in it,
# a shell that renumbers and a shell that does not answer alike.
#
# The third job is held open on a fifo rather than allowed to finish, and the
# question is asked of `jobs` rather than of `wait`, because `wait %1` on a
# job that has already ended is not a question any reference repeats — the
# header says what that costs and why (#2974). Held open, the job is certainly
# in the table, and what `%1` names is then a fact rather than a window:
# either the number came back to the new job or it is still on one of the two
# this file has already waited for, and the two listings are not the same
# text. The status the third job reached is asked of its pid, which is where
# a status is a number rather than a race.
#
# The `jobs` below is the other half of that, and it is what the case was
# missing: holding the third job open makes its presence a fact, and says
# nothing about the *absence* of the jobs this file has already finished
# with. A job that has been waited for has been reaped, but bash keeps its
# entry — `Done`, and still holding its number — until something lists the
# table, so whether `%1` is free when the third job starts is a question
# about what ran earlier in this file rather than about renumbering. bash
# 5.3 answered this line two ways for that reason (#4430): under load the
# background job fifty lines up was still `[1] Done`, the fifo job
# took `[2]`, and `%1` named a job this case has nothing to say about. A
# `jobs` reports the finished entries and drops them, and every job started
# above this line has been waited for — so after it the table holds exactly
# what the shell is still running, and the number the new job takes is a
# fact. It costs the other four references nothing: none of them was still
# holding the entry.
( exit 7 ) & wait $!
( exit 3 ) & c=$!; wait "$c"
jobs > /dev/null 2>&1
mkfifo renumber
{ head -n 1 renumber > /dev/null; exit 4; } &
d=$!
jobs %1 > renum1 2>&1
jobs %% > renumcur 2>&1
printf 'jobspec after a reap -> %s\n' "$( [ "$(cat renum1)" = "$(cat renumcur)" ] && printf 1 || printf 0 )"
printf 'go\n' > renumber
wait "$d"; printf 'and the job it named -> %s\n' "$?"
rm -f renumber renum1 renumcur

# `jobs` runs and says something about nothing; what it prints is a listing
# with pids in it and is a dialect question.
jobs > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'jobs -> %s\n' "$?"

# `fg` is the other half of that: a suite runs with no terminal, so no column
# here has job control, and every one of them refuses. What they refuse with
# is four sentences and two statuses — ksh93 writes nothing at all — so the
# case is whether the shell ran the job, not what it said about not running
# it. `fg` was one of the eleven builtins no case anywhere in this tree
# mentioned (#2293).
( fg ) 2> /dev/null
if [ "$?" -eq 0 ]; then printf 'fg -> ran\n'; else printf 'fg -> refused\n'; fi
( fg %1 ) 2> /dev/null
if [ "$?" -eq 0 ]; then printf 'fg %%1 -> ran\n'; else printf 'fg %%1 -> refused\n'; fi

# The refusal is a diagnostic, so stdout stays empty — which is what a script
# writing `fg 2>/dev/null` is relying on.
printf 'fg stdout [%s]\n' "$( ( fg ) 2> /dev/null )"

# And it is not fatal: the shell carries on past it.
( fg ) 2> /dev/null
printf 'still running after fg\n'

# With a job actually in the table, which is the case this shell got wrong:
# it took `fg` as a live request, named the job on stdout the way an
# interactive shell does, and only then failed (#2657). Every column refuses
# and none of them writes anything on stdout doing it.
#
# `fg` is run directly rather than inside `( )`, and its output is caught in a
# file rather than in `$( )`, because both of those are a subshell and a
# subshell is not where the job is. Wrapped either way the case passes on a
# shell that has the bug, which is the shape of a row that grades nothing.
( exit 0 ) &
fg > fgout 2> /dev/null
if [ "$?" -eq 0 ]; then printf 'fg with a job -> ran\n'; else printf 'fg with a job -> refused\n'; fi
printf 'fg with a job, stdout [%s]\n' "$(cat fgout)"
( exit 0 ) &
fg %1 > fgout1 2> /dev/null
if [ "$?" -eq 0 ]; then printf 'fg %%1 with a job -> ran\n'; else printf 'fg %%1 with a job -> refused\n'; fi
printf 'fg %%1 with a job, stdout [%s]\n' "$(cat fgout1)"
wait

# All of it works inside a function, which is where a script that
# parallelizes actually puts it.
f() { ( exit 2 ) & wait $!; }
f; printf 'in function -> %s\n' "$?"

# ---- what may be put in the background ----

# Every one of these is a job, and `wait` on it reports the status the body
# reached rather than the status of starting it. A pipeline's is its last
# stage's, which is the same rule a foreground pipeline has.
( exit 1 ) | ( exit 5 ) &
wait $!; printf 'a pipeline -> %s\n' "$?"
{ exit 3; } & wait $!; printf 'a group -> %s\n' "$?"
for x in a; do exit 2; done & wait $!; printf 'a for loop -> %s\n' "$?"
true && ( exit 9 ) &
wait $!; printf 'the right side of an and-list -> %s\n' "$?"
( exit 2 ) > /dev/null & wait $!; printf 'with a redirection -> %s\n' "$?"

# A body of nothing but builtins is still a job with a status.
: & wait $!; printf 'a builtin -> %s\n' "$?"
false & wait $!; printf 'a failing builtin -> %s\n' "$?"

# A job started inside a function is the shell's job, and `$!` is set here.
starter() { ( exit 5 ) & }
starter
wait $!; printf 'started in a function -> %s\n' "$?"

# A job started inside a subshell belongs to that subshell: the inner `wait`
# reports it and the outer shell never saw it.
( ( exit 6 ) & wait $!; printf 'the inner shell -> %s\n' "$?" )
printf 'the outer shell -> %s\n' "$?"

# ---- the operands `wait` takes ----

# `%%` and `%+` are both the current job, which is the most recent one, and
# `%-` is the one before it. Two jobs are held open on fifos while the
# question is asked, so both are certainly in the table: nothing has opened
# the other end, so neither can have ended and no reference can have reaped
# one out from under the jobspec.
#
# What the listings are compared against is each other, never printed — a
# `jobs` line is a job number, a state word and a command, and all three are
# spelled differently in every column. Within one shell they are the same
# text for the same job, which is what makes `%+` naming the newer job and
# `%-` naming the older one askable at all.
#
# The statuses come from `wait` on the pids. `wait %%` is what these two
# lines used to be, and it is not askable: it needs the job to have ended
# first, and by then whether the shell still has it is wall-clock. The
# header holds that one out and says what it would have measured (#2974).
mkfifo older newer
{ head -n 1 older > /dev/null; exit 7; } &
o=$!
{ head -n 1 newer > /dev/null; exit 8; } &
n=$!
jobs %% > jcur 2>&1; printf 'current job -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %+ > jplus 2>&1; printf 'plus -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %- > jminus 2>&1; printf 'minus -> %s\n' "$?"
printf 'plus is the current job -> %s\n' "$( [ "$(cat jplus)" = "$(cat jcur)" ] && printf 1 || printf 0 )"
printf 'minus is the other one -> %s\n' "$( [ "$(cat jminus)" = "$(cat jcur)" ] && printf 0 || printf 1 )"
printf 'go\n' > older
printf 'go\n' > newer
wait "$o"; printf 'the older one ended -> %s\n' "$?"
wait "$n"; printf 'the newer one ended -> %s\n' "$?"
rm -f older newer jcur jplus jminus

# A pid that is not a child is 127 — the one status POSIX names for this — and
# a word that is not a pid at all is a refusal the shell carries on past. A
# job specification the shell does not have is held out: ksh93 answers 0 where
# the other three refuse.
wait 999999 2> /dev/null
printf 'a pid that is not ours -> %s\n' "$?"
wait notanumber 2> /dev/null
printf 'a word that is not a pid -> %s\n' "$(( $? != 0 ))"
printf 'still running\n'

# ---- a job's descriptors are the ones it was given ----

# The redirections on a background command apply to it and not to this shell,
# and waiting is what makes reading the file afterwards a fact rather than a
# race.
printf 'hello\n' > infile
{ cat < infile > outfile; } &
wait
printf 'copied [%s]\n' "$(cat outfile)"

# Its standard input is whatever it was redirected from, and a job reading
# from the null device gets an immediate end rather than the shell's input.
{ cat > catout; } < /dev/null &
wait
printf 'read from /dev/null: %s bytes\n' "$(wc -c < catout | tr -d ' ')"

# ---- the rest of the interface ----

# `jobs` takes its two most common options and succeeds; what it prints has
# pids in it and is a dialect question.
( exit 0 ) &
jobs -p > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'jobs -p -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs -l > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'jobs -l -> %s\n' "$?"
wait

# `nohup` runs the command and reports its status, which is the whole of what
# is common about it — where it sends the output when there is a terminal is
# not, and there is no terminal here.
nohup true > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'nohup true -> %s\n' "$?"
nohup false > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'nohup false -> %s\n' "$?"

# A job the shell is still running answers `kill -0`, and stops answering
# once `wait` has reaped it — which is why `kill -0 "$pid"` is not a test for
# "is it still running" in a shell script, and is the same in all four
# columns. The job is held open on a fifo so that the first half is a fact:
# nothing has written to the other end, so the child cannot have ended and
# the pid cannot have gone anywhere.
#
# What this asked before was the harder half — that a child which has
# *exited* and has not been waited for still answers, because nothing has
# reaped it. That is a window rather than a state, and it is not askable
# here at all: every reference reaps a background child from its own SIGCHLD
# handler and not at `wait`, so put anything slow between the `&` and the
# `kill -0` — a builtin-only busy loop is enough — and all four answer 0
# instead. It repeated only because nothing slow happened in between, and
# zsh under load is what slow looks like: it gave both answers about one run
# in forty (#2974, this file's half of #2938). Measured 2026-09-15.
#
# The fifo is `head`'s operand rather than a redirection on the job, and it
# is released with a line rather than with an end of file. Both are the
# file's house spelling and both are explained in the header.
mkfifo held
{ head -n 1 held > /dev/null; exit 0; } &
p=$!
kill -0 "$p" 2> /dev/null
printf 'a running child answers -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
printf 'go\n' > held
wait
kill -0 "$p" 2> /dev/null
printf 'and stops once it is waited for -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
rm -f held

# And it answers however the job came to be blocked. The one above is held by
# the *reader*, which has a process from the moment it starts; this one is
# held by a **redirection on the job**, which every reference opens after it
# has already forked and this shell opens before the job is a process at all.
# That gap was visible from a script: `$!` named something `kill` could not
# reach yet, so the question a script asks before it signals a job answered
# "gone" about a job that was running (#2994). Both spellings, because the
# grammar of the gate is not supposed to be part of the answer — and this is
# the case the other one used to have to be spelled around.
mkfifo redirected
head -n 1 < redirected > /dev/null &
q=$!
kill -0 "$q" 2> /dev/null
printf 'a job blocked in its own redirection answers -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
printf 'go\n' > redirected
wait
kill -0 "$q" 2> /dev/null
printf 'and stops once that one is waited for -> %s\n' "$(( $? == 0 ))"
rm -f redirected

# `kill` on a job specification is not asked: dash refuses every spelling of
# it, which is dash/jobs.tests and is the whole of the difference — ksh93 was
# written down there as refusing `kill -s CONT %1` too, and that turned out
# to be this same reap window read as a dialect answer (#2963). `disown` is
# not asked either: bash and zsh have it and ksh93 and dash do not, which is
# two against two.
